## Why This Is Asked
Tests reasoning with Kleene 3-valued logic, construct a satisfying assignment with U present, and perform constrained backtracking. 

## Key Concepts
Kleene semantics; implication as ¬X ∨ Y; propagation; small depth search.

## Code Example
```javascript
function kleeneNot(x){ if(x==='T') return 'F'; if(x==='F') return 'T'; return 'U'; }
function kleeneOr(a,b){ return a==='T' || b==='T' ? 'T' : a==='U' || b==='U' ? 'U' : 'F'; }
function imply(x,y){ const nx=kleeneNot(x); return kleeneOr(nx,y); }
```

## Why This Is Asked
To gauge practical reasoning about logic constraints, Horn-SAT familiarity, and ability to reason about linear-time algorithms and incremental updates in data pipelines.

## Key Concepts
- Horn clauses
- Forward chaining
- Partial vs full assignments
- Complexity O(n+m)
- Cycle handling and inconsistency detection

## Code Example
```javascript
function solveHorn(rules, facts){
  const trueSet = new Set(facts);
  let queue = rules.filter(r => r.antecedent.every(v => trueSet.has(v)));
  while (queue.length){
    const r = queue.shift();
    if (!trueSet.has(r.consequent)){
      trueSet.add(r.consequent);
      queue = rules.filter(x => x.antecedent.every(v => trueSet.has(v)) && !trueSet.has(x.consequent));
    }
  }
  return trueSet;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to non-Horn clauses?
- How would you handle incremental updates when facts change?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason with Kleene 3-valued logic in a realistic policy-engine style, including short-circuit semantics and propagation.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic: ¬ flips T/F, U→U; ∧/∨ follow min/max with T>U>F; A→B as ¬A ∨ B.
- Short-circuit evaluation in rule engines.
- Backtracking propagation for constraint satisfaction in a 3-valued setting.

## Code Example
```javascript
function kleeneNot(a){ return a === 'T' ? 'F' : a === 'F' ? 'T' : 'U' }
function kleeneOr(a,b){ /* min/max with T>U>F */ return (a === 'T' || b === 'T') ? 'T' : (a === 'U' || b === 'U') ? 'U' : 'F' }
function kleeneAnd(a,b){ return (a === 'F' || b === 'F') ? 'F' : (a === 'U' || b === 'U') ? 'U' : 'T' }
function implies(a,b){ // ¬a ∨ b
  const na = kleeneNot(a)
  return kleeneOr(na, b)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does the answer change if short-circuiting is disabled? 
- How would you extend to detect unsatisfiable sets with more variables?

