## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a small CSP with Kleene semantics, reason about propagation, and craft a concrete counterfactual assignment with U, plus depth-limited reasoning.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic: T>U>F; ¬ maps T↔F, U↔U.
- Implication as ¬X ∨ Y; F only when antecedent is T and consequent F.
- Depth-limited backtracking with constraint propagation to avoid exploring futile branches.

## Code Example
```javascript
function kleeneNot(v){ return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U';}
function kleeneOr(a,b){ return a==='T'||b==='T'?'T': a==='U'||b==='U'?'U':'F';}
function kleeneImp(a,b){ // a -> b
  if(a==='T' && b==='F') return 'F';
  if(a==='T' && b==='U') return 'U';
  if(a==='F') return 'T';
  if(a==='U' && b==='F') return 'U';
  return (b==='T') ? 'T' : 'U';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would adding a 6th variable complicate backtracking?
- How to prove optimality of the search depth?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to reason about combined constraints and partial knowledge in a policy graph using three-valued logic; requires identifying a concrete satisfying assignment and developing a general propagation strategy for 3-valued logic with conjunction and implication operations.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic semantics for implication (→), conjunction (∧), and disjunction (∨)
- Propagation of unknown (U) values through mixed logical operators
- Fixpoint computation with backtracking to avoid false (F) while preserving unknowns

## Code Example

```javascript
function imp(a,b){ // a->b in Kleene logic
  const not = v => v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U';
  const or  = (x,y) => (x==='T' || y==='T') ? 'T' : (x==='U' || y==='U') ? 'U' : 'F';
  return or(not(a), b);
}

function propagate(rules, values){
  let changed;
  do{
    changed = false;
    for(const [name, expr] of rules){
      const newVal = expr(values);
      if(values[name] !== newVal){
        values[name] = newVal;
        changed = true;
      }
    }
  } while(changed);
  return values;
}
```

The algorithm iteratively propagates known values through constraints until a fixpoint is reached, ensuring no constraint evaluates to F while maintaining at least one U value.
